<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Alan Kingdom in Hispania</h1><p class="st-meta">409 CE – 418 CE (approx.) · capital Emerita Augusta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived realm of the Iranian-speaking Alans in Lusitania and Carthaginiensis, destroyed by the Visigoths within a decade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a>, nomads from the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 406 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 409. In 411, when the invaders divided the provinces by lot, the Alans took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carthaginiensis">Carthaginiensis</a>, the largest share.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They formed a warrior aristocracy ruling over a Hispano-Roman population, living from tribute and plunder rather than building lasting institutions. Their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addac">Addax</a> led them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. Very little is known of their administration, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a> is the main source.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 416 the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallia">Wallia</a>, acting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, campaigned against them, and in 418 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addac">Addax</a> was killed. The surviving <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Hasding Vandals</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunderic">Gunderic</a>, whose kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> styled themselves kings of the Vandals and Alans.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 409 CE – 418 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.84%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>409 CE – 418 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Emerita Augusta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Addax</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alans in Lusitania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=414&amp;focus=alan-kingdom-in-hispania" aria-label="Alan Kingdom in Hispania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4591 747 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alan Kingdom in Hispania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4911 1003h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4911 1003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=414&amp;focus=alan-kingdom-in-hispania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
